---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const seqCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '2 + 3*x', label: 'arithmetic: 2 + 3n', color: '#7c3aed' },
    { expr: '2*1.4^x', label: 'geometric: 2 × 1.4ⁿ', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'n (term number)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 40 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};

const gaussCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'S = 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 100', note: 'Gauss, age 9, is asked to add the first hundred numbers. Brute force works; insight is faster.' },
    { math: 'S = 100 + 99 + … + 1', note: 'Write the same sum backwards underneath.' },
    { math: '2S = 101 × 100', note: 'Add vertically: every column pairs to 101, and there are 100 columns.' },
    { math: 'S = 100 × 101 / 2 = 5050', note: 'The pairing trick generalises: 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2 — a formula earned in three lines.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/intermediate/sequences-and-series">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A sequence is a list with a rule; a <strong>series</strong> is what happens when you add it
    up. Two families dominate. <strong>Arithmetic sequences</strong> add a fixed amount each
    step — steady, linear, predictable. <strong>Geometric sequences</strong> multiply by a fixed
    factor — slow at first, then unstoppable. The difference between them is the difference
    between savings with flat interest and savings with compound interest.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="linear vs multiplicative growth — the geometric curve is barely visible until suddenly it isn't" widget="plotter" config={seqCfg} />

  <p>
    The arithmetic line overtakes early and looks comfortable. The geometric curve hides below
    it for years, then doubles past it. Every "exponential surprise" in finance, epidemics or
    technology is this picture.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="Gauss's pairing trick — the sum of 1 to 100 in three moves" widget="equationStep" config={gaussCfg} />

  <h2>The working formulas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Arithmetic sum:</strong> S = n × (first + last)/2 — average of first and last, times how many. Pairs, not grinding.</li>
    <li><strong>Geometric sum:</strong> S = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) for r ≠ 1 — derived by the same trick: subtract the shifted series from itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Infinite geometric:</strong> when the ratio magnitude is below 1 the sum converges: S = a/(1 − r). This one runs Zeno's paradoxes, repeating decimals and drug-dosing schedules alike.</li>
    <li><strong>The warning:</strong> a geometric series with r > 1 has no finite sum — which is the mathematical voice behind every compound-debt warning.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Algebra and Trigonometry (Sequences); standard treatments (Stewart, Precalculus).</p>
</TopicPage>
